WB.L("nuremberg-trials", "society", "advanced", "The Nuremberg trials and the birth of international criminal law", "In 1945 the leaders of a defeated regime were put on trial before the world for crimes so vast there were barely words for them. The Nuremberg trials invented much of modern international law, and their critics raised questions we are still answering.",
  [
    ["A courtroom in the ruins", "In November 1945, surviving Nazi leaders went on trial in the bombed city of Nuremberg.", "On 20 November 1945, in a courtroom in the Bavarian city of Nuremberg, twenty-one men sat in two rows of a wooden dock. Among them were Hermann Göring, once Hitler's designated successor, the former deputy leader Rudolf Hess and the foreign minister Joachim von Ribbentrop. Much of the city around them lay in ruins. Nuremberg was chosen partly because its Palace of Justice had survived the bombing with a prison attached, and partly for its symbolism: it was where the Nazi Party had held its huge rallies. For the first time, the leaders of a modern state were being tried before an international tribunal for crimes committed in waging war and against civilians. Interpreters wearing headsets translated proceedings simultaneously into four languages, a novelty at the time."],
    ["Why a trial at all?", "The Allies chose a trial over summary execution, partly to create a lasting record.", "As the war ended, Allied leaders debated what to do with captured Nazi leaders. Some, including at times Winston Churchill, favoured simply shooting the most senior figures once identified. Others, especially in the United States, argued for a trial. A courtroom would test the evidence, give the accused a chance to defend themselves and build a public record that could not easily be denied later. In August 1945 the United States, Britain, France and the Soviet Union signed the London Charter, creating the International Military Tribunal and defining the crimes it would judge. The American chief prosecutor, Supreme Court Justice Robert H. Jackson, said in his opening address that it was a great tribute to reason that the victors stayed their hand and submitted their enemies to the judgment of the law."],
    ["New crimes, new words", "The tribunal judged conspiracy, crimes against peace, war crimes and crimes against humanity.", "The London Charter listed the charges. Crimes against peace covered planning and waging wars of aggression. War crimes covered breaches of the laws of war, such as murdering prisoners or civilians in occupied territory. Crimes against humanity covered murder, extermination, enslavement, deportation and persecution of civilian populations. A further count of conspiracy tied these together. The idea of crimes against humanity was especially important, because it reached acts a government committed against its own people, which traditional international law had largely ignored. A related word was also entering use. In 1944 the Polish-Jewish lawyer Raphael Lemkin had coined the term genocide to describe the deliberate destruction of a people. It was mentioned at Nuremberg, and in 1948 the United Nations adopted a convention against it."],
    ["Evidence and verdicts", "Most defendants were convicted, but three were acquitted.", "The prosecution relied heavily on the Nazis' own documents, including orders, minutes and reports, as well as film shot by Allied troops in liberated concentration camps. Survivors and former officials testified. The trial lasted almost a year. On 1 October 1946 the judges delivered their verdicts. Twelve defendants were sentenced to death, including Martin Bormann, who was tried in his absence. Seven received prison terms, from ten years to life. Three were acquitted, which surprised many observers and showed the judges were not simply rubber-stamping the prosecution. The tribunal also rejected the defence of superior orders: following orders could reduce a sentence but did not excuse a crime. Göring took his own life the night before the executions, which were carried out on 16 October 1946."],
    ["The case against Nuremberg", "Critics argued the trials were victors' justice and applied some laws retrospectively.", "Even at the time, serious people had doubts. The most common criticism was victors' justice: only the defeated were tried, while Allied actions, such as the area bombing of German cities, were never examined. The Soviet Union's presence was particularly awkward, since it had invaded Poland in 1939 alongside Germany, and the Soviet prosecutors tried to blame the Katyn massacre of Polish officers on the Germans, though the Soviets were responsible. The tribunal did not convict on that charge. Others argued that crimes against peace had not clearly been crimes when the acts were committed, breaching the principle that no one should be punished under a law made after the fact. Defenders replied that aggression had already been outlawed by treaty and that the atrocities were crimes under any civilised legal system."],
    ["From Nuremberg to The Hague", "The trials laid the groundwork for later international courts, including the International Criminal Court.", "Nuremberg was followed by other trials. The International Military Tribunal for the Far East tried Japanese leaders in Tokyo, with the Australian judge Sir William Webb as its president. American military courts at Nuremberg held twelve further trials of doctors, judges, industrialists and others. The doctors' trial produced the Nuremberg Code, a set of principles for ethical medical research. In 1950 the United Nations' International Law Commission summarised the Nuremberg Principles. Then the Cold War largely froze the project for decades. It revived in the 1990s, when the United Nations created tribunals for the former Yugoslavia and Rwanda. In 1998 countries adopted the Rome Statute, and in 2002 the permanent International Criminal Court began work in The Hague. Australia ratified the Rome Statute in 2002."],
    ["Questions that remain", "Nuremberg established that individuals can answer for state crimes, but enforcement is still uneven.", "Nuremberg's most lasting principle is simple to state: individuals, even heads of state, can be held personally responsible for the gravest crimes, and acting on behalf of a government is no shield. That idea is now part of international law. Yet putting it into practice remains difficult. International courts depend on countries to arrest suspects and hand them over, and several major powers, including the United States, China and Russia, are not members of the International Criminal Court. Supporters argue that imperfect justice is far better than none and that the threat of prosecution can deter atrocities. Sceptics worry about selective enforcement and political influence. Both sides tend to agree, though, that the questions first asked in that Nuremberg courtroom are far from settled."]
  ],
  [
    [1, "Which four powers signed the London Charter creating the Nuremberg tribunal?", ["The US, Britain, Canada and Australia", "The US, Britain, France and the Soviet Union", "Britain, France, China and the US", "The US, the Soviet Union, Poland and Britain"], 1, "These four Allied powers set up the International Military Tribunal in August 1945."],
    [2, "What made the charge of crimes against humanity especially significant?", ["It applied only to soldiers", "It replaced the laws of war", "It could reach acts a government committed against its own people", "It covered only economic crimes"], 2, "Traditional international law had largely ignored how states treated their own citizens."],
    [3, "How did the tribunal treat the defence of following superior orders?", ["As a complete excuse", "As irrelevant to sentencing", "As grounds for automatic acquittal", "As not excusing a crime, though it could reduce a sentence"], 3, "Following orders could mitigate punishment but did not remove guilt."],
    [4, "What is the criticism known as victors' justice?", ["Only the defeated side's actions were put on trial", "The judges were all German", "The trials were held in secret", "No one was convicted"], 0, "Allied conduct, such as area bombing, was never examined."],
    [5, "Which Australian judge presided over the Tokyo war crimes tribunal?", ["Sir Owen Dixon", "Sir Garfield Barwick", "Sir Isaac Isaacs", "Sir William Webb"], 3, "Webb was president of the International Military Tribunal for the Far East."]
  ]);

WB.L("votes-for-women-au", "society", "beginner", "Votes for women: how Australia led the world", "In the 1890s, a colony on the edge of the British Empire did something almost no government on Earth had done: it let women vote and stand for parliament. The story of how Australian women won the vote is full of determined campaigners, giant petitions and some uncomfortable exceptions.",
  [
    ["A petition 260 metres long", "Campaigners gathered tens of thousands of signatures on enormous petitions.", "In 1891 Victorian campaigners presented their parliament with a petition so long it was rolled up like a carpet. Known as the Monster Petition, it carried around 30,000 signatures, gathered door to door by women across the colony asking for the right to vote. It did not succeed straight away, but it showed something politicians could not ignore: huge numbers of ordinary women wanted a say. Similar petitions were organised across the Australian colonies in the 1880s and 1890s. Campaigners held public meetings, wrote to newspapers and lobbied members of parliament one by one. Many were involved in other social causes too, especially the Woman's Christian Temperance Union, which campaigned against alcohol abuse and saw the vote as a way for women to protect their families and communities."],
    ["South Australia goes first", "In 1894 South Australia gave women both the vote and the right to stand for parliament.", "New Zealand became the first self-governing country to give women the vote in national elections, in 1893. South Australia followed in December 1894 and went one step further: its law let women both vote and stand as candidates for parliament, a first anywhere in the world. Campaigners such as Mary Lee, an Irish-born widow who threw herself into the cause, had worked for years to get there. A petition with more than 11,000 signatures was presented to the South Australian parliament in 1894. Opponents had hoped that adding the right to stand for parliament would make the bill too radical to pass. The tactic backfired, and the bill passed with that right included. South Australian women voted for the first time in 1896."],
    ["The arguments of the time", "Supporters and opponents argued about women's nature, family and fairness.", "It helps to understand the arguments people actually made. Opponents claimed that politics was too rough for women, that women's influence belonged in the home, that wives would simply copy their husbands' votes or, oddly, that they would cause conflict in marriages by voting differently. Some worried that women would vote as a single bloc on issues like alcohol. Supporters answered that women paid taxes and obeyed the laws, so they deserved a say in making them. Others argued that women would bring a moral and caring perspective to public life, while some simply insisted that equal citizenship was a matter of basic justice. These debates were not neatly divided along modern party lines, and supporters and opponents came from across the political spectrum."],
    ["A nation is born, with a catch", "The Commonwealth Franchise Act 1902 gave most women the federal vote but excluded most Aboriginal people.", "When the colonies federated in 1901, the new national Parliament had to decide who could vote in federal elections. The Commonwealth Franchise Act 1902 gave women the right to vote and stand for federal Parliament on the same terms as men, making Australia one of the first nations in the world to do so. But the same Act excluded most Aboriginal people, as well as people from Asia, Africa and the Pacific Islands other than New Zealand, unless they were already entitled to vote in their state. In practice, many Aboriginal people were denied the vote for decades. Aboriginal people gained the clear right to enrol for federal elections in 1962, and enrolment and voting became compulsory for them, as for everyone else, in 1984."],
    ["The first candidates", "Four women stood for federal Parliament in 1903, but none were elected.", "At the 1903 federal election, four women nominated as candidates, the first women anywhere in the British Empire to stand for a national parliament. The best known was Vida Goldstein, a Melbourne campaigner who ran for the Senate in Victoria and won tens of thousands of votes, though not enough to be elected. The others were Selina Anderson, Nellie Martel and Mary Moore-Bentley. Goldstein stood several more times without success. Catherine Helen Spence, a writer and reformer from South Australia, had already made history in 1897 when she stood for the convention that drafted the Constitution, becoming Australia's first female political candidate. Their campaigns showed women could take part fully in politics, even if voters were slow to elect them."],
    ["A slow road into parliament", "Winning the vote came long before women were elected in significant numbers.", "The states gave women the vote at different times. Western Australia followed South Australia in 1899, and Victoria was the last state, in 1908. Being allowed to stand was one thing; being elected was another. The first woman elected to any Australian parliament was Edith Cowan, who won a seat in the Western Australian Legislative Assembly in 1921. Federally, it took until 1943 for the first women to be elected: Enid Lyons in the House of Representatives and Dorothy Tangney in the Senate. They came from opposite sides of politics, Lyons from the conservative United Australia Party and Tangney from Labor. For decades afterward, women remained a small minority in Australian parliaments, and their numbers grew significantly only from the late 20th century."],
    ["Why it matters now", "Australia's early start is a point of pride, and a reminder that rights can be partial.", "Australia's suffrage story is often told as a simple triumph, and there is real reason for pride. A young democracy on the far side of the world helped lead a global change that took many countries, including Britain and the United States, decades longer. Yet the full story is more complicated. The same laws that welcomed many women into political life shut out most Aboriginal women and men. Recognising both parts lets us see that rights are rarely won all at once. Today, voting is compulsory for all adult citizens, and you can check your enrolment on the Australian Electoral Commission's website. Every time you cast a ballot, you are using a right that people campaigned for, petition by petition, for decades."]
  ],
  [
    [0, "What was the 1891 Victorian Monster Petition?", ["A petition against federation", "A petition of around 30,000 signatures for women's right to vote", "A petition to ban alcohol", "A petition for compulsory voting"], 1, "It was gathered door to door and presented to the Victorian parliament."],
    [1, "What did South Australia's 1894 law achieve that was a world first?", ["Compulsory voting", "Secret ballots", "Votes for 18-year-olds", "Allowing women to stand as candidates for parliament"], 3, "New Zealand had given women the vote in 1893, but South Australia also let them stand."],
    [3, "Who was largely excluded by the Commonwealth Franchise Act 1902?", ["Most Aboriginal people", "Women over 30", "Landowners", "People born in Britain"], 0, "Most Aboriginal people were denied the federal vote unless already enrolled in their state."],
    [4, "Which campaigner stood for the Senate in Victoria in 1903?", ["Edith Cowan", "Enid Lyons", "Vida Goldstein", "Dorothy Tangney"], 2, "Goldstein was one of four women who stood for federal Parliament in 1903."],
    [5, "Who was the first woman elected to any Australian parliament?", ["Mary Lee", "Catherine Helen Spence", "Enid Lyons", "Edith Cowan"], 3, "Cowan won a seat in the Western Australian Legislative Assembly in 1921."]
  ]);

WB.L("social-contract", "society", "advanced", "The social contract: why we agree to be governed", "Why should anyone obey the government? For nearly four centuries, philosophers have answered with a powerful thought experiment: imagine life with no rulers at all, then ask what deal reasonable people would strike.",
  [
    ["Imagine no government", "Social contract thinkers begin by imagining life without any state.", "Picture waking up tomorrow to find there are no police, no courts, no laws and no government of any kind. No one would stop a stronger neighbour from taking your food or your home. Would people cooperate peacefully, or would fear take over? This imagined situation is called the state of nature, and it is the starting point for one of the most influential ideas in political philosophy: the social contract. The idea is that governments are legitimate because, in some sense, the people they govern have agreed to them, trading some freedom for security and order. The great social contract thinkers disagreed sharply about what the state of nature would be like, and that disagreement shaped very different views about how much power governments should have."],
    ["Hobbes and the war of all against all", "Thomas Hobbes argued that without a powerful ruler, life would be constant fear and conflict.", "The English philosopher Thomas Hobbes lived through the violence of the English Civil War, and it shows. In Leviathan, published in 1651, he argued that in a state of nature people would be roughly equal in their ability to harm one another, and constantly suspicious. The result would be a war of every person against every person. There would be no farming, no industry, no arts and no society, and, in his famous phrase, the life of man would be solitary, poor, nasty, brutish, and short. The only escape, Hobbes said, was for people to hand power to a single sovereign strong enough to keep the peace. He believed even a harsh ruler was better than the chaos of no rule at all."],
    ["Locke and natural rights", "John Locke argued that people have rights before government exists, and governments must protect them.", "John Locke, writing a few decades later, painted a gentler picture. In his Two Treatises of Government, published around 1689, he argued that even in a state of nature people are bound by a natural law that forbids harming others in their life, health, liberty or possessions. People form governments not to escape total chaos, but to protect these rights more reliably, with impartial judges and enforcement. That changes everything. If a government is created to protect rights, it can lose its legitimacy by violating them. Locke argued that people could then resist and replace it. His ideas about consent and natural rights echoed through the American Declaration of Independence in 1776, which speaks of unalienable rights to life, liberty and the pursuit of happiness."],
    ["Rousseau and the general will", "Jean-Jacques Rousseau argued that a legitimate society must express the shared will of its citizens.", "The Geneva-born thinker Jean-Jacques Rousseau took the idea in a different direction. His book The Social Contract, published in 1762, opens with the famous line usually translated as 'Man is born free, and everywhere he is in chains.' Rousseau thought existing societies had corrupted people and entrenched inequality. A legitimate society, he argued, would be one where citizens collectively make the laws they live under, guided by the general will: what is genuinely in the common interest, not just the sum of private wants. In obeying such laws, people would in effect be obeying themselves. His ideas inspired democratic and revolutionary movements, especially in France. Critics, though, worried that the notion of a general will could be twisted to justify forcing people to conform."],
    ["But did anyone actually sign?", "Critics point out that no real person ever agreed to the social contract.", "The obvious objection is that no one ever signed anything. You were born into a country with laws already in place. The Scottish philosopher David Hume made this point sharply in the 18th century, arguing that most governments began in conquest or usurpation, not agreement. Some contract theorists reply that by staying in a country and accepting its benefits, you give tacit consent. Hume answered with a memorable comparison: saying a poor labourer has consented to the state because he could leave is like saying a man consented to a ship's captain because he could jump overboard. Later critics went further. Some feminist and other scholars argued that historical versions of the contract quietly excluded many people, including women and colonised peoples, from the agreement altogether."],
    ["Rawls and the veil of ignorance", "John Rawls revived the social contract as a test for fairness rather than history.", "In 1971 the American philosopher John Rawls breathed new life into the idea with A Theory of Justice. Rawls did not claim anyone had made a real contract. Instead he asked what principles people would choose if they were designing a society from behind a veil of ignorance, not knowing whether they would be rich or poor, talented or not, or which gender, race or religion they would have. Rawls argued they would choose equal basic liberties for all and would accept inequalities only if they benefited the least advantaged. Not everyone agreed. His Harvard colleague Robert Nozick argued in 1974 that people's rights to what they have earned strictly limit what the state may redistribute, defending a much smaller government."],
    ["Why it still matters", "Social contract ideas sit quietly underneath many modern political arguments.", "You may never use the phrase, but social contract thinking is everywhere. When people argue that taxes are the price of a civilised society, or that government has broken faith with its citizens, they are drawing on it. Debates about how much freedom to trade for safety, from lockdowns to surveillance, echo the argument between Hobbes and Locke. Arguments over welfare and redistribution often replay Rawls and Nozick. The theories do not settle these questions, and thinkers on many sides of politics have used contract ideas to support very different conclusions. What they offer is a useful habit of mind: when someone claims authority over you, ask what would make that authority reasonable to accept, and whether the people affected could genuinely agree to it."]
  ],
  [
    [0, "What is the state of nature in social contract theory?", ["An imagined situation with no government at all", "A national park system", "The natural environment before humans", "A form of direct democracy"], 0, "Thinkers use it as a starting point to ask why government is justified."],
    [1, "How did Hobbes describe life in the state of nature?", ["Peaceful and cooperative", "Solitary, poor, nasty, brutish, and short", "Free but unequal", "Simple and happy"], 1, "Hobbes thought only a powerful sovereign could prevent constant conflict."],
    [2, "According to Locke, what can people do if a government violates their natural rights?", ["Nothing, since all rule is legitimate", "Only petition the monarch", "Resist and replace it", "Leave the country"], 2, "Locke tied legitimacy to protecting rights, so violating them could forfeit it."],
    [4, "What point did David Hume make with his ship comparison?", ["Ships need strong captains", "Citizens should emigrate freely", "Governments are like businesses", "Staying in a country is not real consent if leaving is not a realistic choice"], 3, "Hume doubted that tacit consent showed genuine agreement."],
    [5, "What is Rawls's veil of ignorance designed to test?", ["Which principles of justice people would choose without knowing their place in society", "How much people know about politics", "Whether voters are well informed", "How secret ballots work"], 0, "Not knowing your position pushes you to choose principles fair to everyone."]
  ]);
